 "finding": "The Sacsayhuamán pass lays onto the ground to within its fitted offset of 10.0 m along track and 12.2 m across (image against predicted brightness, correlation 0.09); the image covers 100% of the site. The published method, run over the zigzag walls, the Rodadero outcrop, open fields and three stretches of houses, draws its columns wherever the registration wanders (the power at a pixel follows its trajectory's energy at 1.0000 or better). Its mean depth profiles correlate at 0.84 to 1.00 across the six areas. The walls' correlates with the houses' at 0.88 to 0.89 and with the fields' at 0.84, while the houses' correlate with one another at 0.98 to 1.00 and the Rodadero's with the houses' and fields' at 0.93 to 0.97. By the test stated before the run, the monuments stand apart: every pairing of a monument with houses or fields is less alike than any two stretches of houses, the walls by a wide margin and the Rodadero only just. The strongest columns go to the houses: 8 to 12% of their pixels are among the noisiest 5% of all, against 0.0% at most of the monuments' and 0.9% of the fields'; the typical pixel's wander differs by 153% across the six, the walls' the quietest. The areas' motionless copies, each area's brightness pattern and spectrum with fresh speckle and nothing moving, keep neither the shared profile nor the walls' departure from it: the copies' profiles correlate with the real ones at -0.05 to 0.41 and with one another at -0.22 to 0.50. Both come from something the real image holds beyond its brightness pattern; this run does not say what, and it is not below the surface, which the X-band wave reaches no further into than about 0.3 m (P2-11)."
